# .public-surface-allow - justified exceptions to scripts/public-surface-check.
#
# One rule per line, four fields:
#
#     category | path-glob | reason | pattern
#
# A rule masks exactly the text its pattern matches, only under the paths its
# glob matches, only for its category. Everything else on the same line is
# still checked, so an exception can never quietly widen into a hole, and a
# rule broad enough to switch a category off is rejected rather than obeyed.
#
# Patterns use %HH hex escapes where spelling a term in full would otherwise
# trip the guard's own scan of this file.
#
# Run `scripts/public-surface-check --help` for the full syntax.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The public API and product this repository exists to publish.
#
# The schema, the package on npm, the product it is published under and the
# key prefix it writes in a browser are all names a stranger has to be able to
# read, type and search for. Each spelling gets its own rule: the separator
# class covers the dotted schema package, the underscored generated
# identifiers, the hyphenated package and the spaced brand, and nothing else.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
codename | * | the schema package this repository is published to distribute, in every spelling the generated bindings use | %6dedallion[ ._-]terminal
private-repo | * | the published npm package name, the browser storage key prefix it owns and the example dashboard named after it are public identifiers a host has to be able to find | %6dedallion-terminal(:|'|"|\.json)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Third-party product vocabulary. These clones reproduce other companies' user
# interfaces so this toolkit can be judged against them, so they carry those
# companies' product names. The names are theirs, not ours.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
codename | examples/clones/palantir/* | a clone of the Palantir product of that name, down to its CSS class names; the product is theirs | com%70ass
codename | src/__tests__/operationalProductShowcases.test.tsx | this suite drives the Palantir clone above and names the surface it is asserting on | com%70ass
codename | examples/clones/* | Palantir's object-model product is called this; the clone reproduces its navigation labels | Onto%6cogy

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The generic word for an object model. This toolkit renders object types,
# properties, links and actions, and that data model is what the industry
# calls an ontology; the word is capitalised here only because it opens a
# sentence, a table cell or a UI label. The lower-case deny patterns still
# run, so a product-qualified name such as the service form is still caught.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
codename | *.md | the generic term for the object model this toolkit renders, capitalised at the head of a sentence or a table cell | Onto%6cogy
codename | proto/* | the generic term for the object model, capitalised at the head of a schema comment | Onto%6cogy
codename | */gen/* | generated bindings carry the schema comments through verbatim | Onto%6cogy
codename | src/*.tsx | the generic term for the object model, as a gallery title and as test fixture data | Onto%6cogy
